# hashi — reverse proxy.
#
# WHAT: TLS termination in front of the control plane, split into a PUBLIC site
#       that serves nothing but `/sub/*` and a PRIVATE site that serves the panel
#       and the API.
# WHY:  the access log is disabled deliberately. Request paths contain
#       subscription tokens, and a subscription token is a bearer credential —
#       logging one writes a live key to disk. See
#       .claude/projects/04-infra-rulebook.md (Traps) and the matching rule in
#       the control zone: both are needed, neither is sufficient alone.
#
#       EVERY site block below discards its own log, and a new one that forgets to
#       is a credential leak rather than an untidy file. tests/infra/test_caddyfile.py
#       reads this file and fails on a block without it — that check exists because
#       M7 turned one site block into three, and "remember to add the log block" is
#       not a control.
#
#       The split is M7. The panel holds every node key and every user
#       credential, and `control/app/auth.py` deliberately has no lockout on failed
#       logins, so password length was the entire defence for a surface anyone on
#       the internet could reach. `/sub/{token}` cannot move with it: a user
#       refreshes their config from inside Russia at the exact moment a node was
#       blocked, so that route stays on the public name, unchanged.
# HOW:  the admin endpoint stays on loopback so the healthcheck can reach it
#       without exposing configuration to the network.
{
	email {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL}
	admin 127.0.0.1:2019

	# Automatic HTTP→HTTPS redirects are OFF, and the public one below is written
	# out by hand instead. Not a style choice: with them on, Caddy derives a
	# redirect for EVERY site name it serves, including the private one — so port
	# 80, which faces the whole internet, answers a request carrying the private
	# host header with `Location: https://<private host>:<private port>/…` and
	# hands out the address of the console this milestone just took off the public
	# internet. Measured against caddy:2.8.4-alpine, not assumed.
	#
	# ACME is unaffected: the HTTP challenge is answered by the TLS app before any
	# site route is consulted, so the explicit redirect site cannot shadow it.
	auto_https disable_redirects

	# What to serve a client that sends no SNI at all — and that is not an exotic
	# client, it is every ordinary one. **A TLS client omits SNI whenever the host is
	# an IP literal**: curl does, every browser does, and the specification tells them
	# to. A management tunnel hands the owner an ADDRESS rather than a name, so the
	# console is dialled by IP by definition, and the SSH fallback is dialled at
	# 127.0.0.1 by construction.
	#
	# Without this line, a listener carrying more than one certificate has no name to
	# choose between them and Caddy aborts with `tlsv1 alert internal error`. That is
	# not a theory: M7 shipped without it, passed review, and the console could not be
	# reached on the first real host by any route the owner has — not over the tunnel,
	# not over the forward. The suite could not see it because the test stack used a
	# NAME for the console, and a name sends SNI.
	#
	# The value is the CONSOLE's name, not the delivery endpoint's, and that choice
	# costs something worth stating. It is global — Caddy has no per-site form of it,
	# checked against 2.8.4 — so a no-SNI connection to the public 443 is also answered
	# with the console's certificate, which discloses the console's (private,
	# unroutable) address to anyone who asks for it that way. The alternative was to
	# default to the public name, which discloses nothing and leaves the owner facing a
	# certificate-name mismatch on every single visit to their own console. Training the
	# one operator to click through TLS warnings on the one surface that must never be
	# impersonated is the worse of the two, by some distance. An operator who can
	# resolve a name on their laptop AND their phone can set HASHI_PRIVATE_HOST to that
	# name instead and be left with neither cost.
	default_sni {$HASHI_PRIVATE_HOST}

	# Silences the DEFAULT logger, which is not the same thing as the per-site
	# ones below. Without this, any request that does not match a site block —
	# by IP, or by another name resolving here — is written to stderr with its
	# full URI, and so is every http.log.error (a 502 while the control plane
	# restarts is exactly when a user is refreshing a subscription). Both
	# paths end up in the container log on disk, and the URI contains a live
	# subscription token.
	log {
		output discard
	}
}

# The public name, on port 80. Exists only to send a browser to https — it proxies
# nothing and therefore reaches no route. Written out because `auto_https
# disable_redirects` above turns the automatic version off for every name at once,
# and this is the one name that should still have it.
http://{$HASHI_PUBLIC_HOST} {
	log {
		output discard
	}

	redir https://{host}{uri} 308
}

# PUBLIC — the delivery endpoint, and nothing else.
#
# A user inside Russia reaches this and only this. Anything that is not a
# subscription is 404, including `/`, `/healthz` and `/panel/…`: a 404 says the
# same thing to a scanner as it does to a mistyped URL, and it says nothing about
# what else this host runs.
{$HASHI_PUBLIC_HOST} {
	log {
		output discard
	}

	# WHO ISSUES THIS NAME'S CERTIFICATE, and the default is nobody on the internet — M31.1.
	# `internal` (Caddy's own CA) unless HASHI_PUBLIC_TLS says otherwise, and the only
	# file that says otherwise is deploy/docker-compose.public.yml, which sets it to the
	# ACME account's email: that is the overlay that publishes 80 and 443, so it is the
	# one shape in which a CA's validation can reach this container at all.
	#
	# The base stack publishes nothing, and it is what `make panel-bootstrap` and
	# `make restore-from-bucket` bring up. With automatic HTTPS left to decide, Caddy
	# began public issuance for this name the moment that stack booted — a restore walk
	# found Let's Encrypt and ZeroSSL accounts, an issuance lock and a challenge token
	# in /data/caddy/acme — and every validation that cannot succeed counts against the
	# live domain's rate limits, during a disaster restore of all moments. An unpublished
	# stack serving this name from its own CA serves nobody either way; the difference
	# is that it asks nothing of anyone.
	#
	# A switch in the environment rather than a second Caddyfile, because the image
	# carries exactly one Caddyfile (deploy/docker/caddy.Dockerfile) and a second copy of
	# this block is a second place for the delivery rules below to drift. Rejected:
	# `auto_https off` in the base shape — it switches off the HTTP redirect site and the
	# console's own certificate too, which the base stack still serves inside its network.
	tls {$HASHI_PUBLIC_TLS:internal}

	@delivery path /sub/*
	handle @delivery {
		reverse_proxy control:8000
	}

	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}

# PRIVATE — the panel and the API, on a port that is published only on the
# management tunnel's interface and on loopback (deploy/docker-compose.public.yml).
#
# The interface restriction lives in the PUBLICATION, not here: a `bind` directive
# in this file is evaluated inside the container's own network namespace, where
# the tunnel's address does not exist, so it would restrict nothing while looking
# like it did. Caddy listens on this port inside the container; the host decides
# which address the port appears on.
#
# Two addresses, one block, on purpose. The first is how the owner reaches the
# panel over the management tunnel. The second is the way back in when that tunnel
# is unavailable — `ssh -L` forwards the same port to the host's loopback, and the
# request then arrives with `127.0.0.1` as its name. Both are served the same
# content by the same rules; the difference is entirely in which host address the
# port is published on. See docs/deploy/runbook.md, "Reaching the panel".
{$HASHI_PRIVATE_HOST}:{$HASHI_PRIVATE_PORT}, https://127.0.0.1:{$HASHI_PRIVATE_PORT} {
	log {
		output discard
	}

	# Caddy's own CA, never a public one. This site has no public name and no
	# public address, so ACME cannot validate it — a public issuer would retry
	# forever and the panel would never be served, which is the same failure the
	# e2e stack hit for its own reason (docs/decisions.md, 2026-08-08). The owner
	# accepts this certificate once, or trusts the container's root; either way
	# nothing about the console's existence is published to a CA's logs.
	tls internal

	# `/static/*` belongs here rather than on the public site: it is the panel's
	# own stylesheet, fonts and one script, and nothing a user fetches.
	@console path /panel/* /api/* /static/*
	handle @console {
		reverse_proxy control:8000
	}

	handle {
		respond 404
	}
}
